In this lesson we'll look at tree diagrams, a really handy way to picture probability when an experiment happens in stages. Picture a tree growing to the right. At each step, you draw a branch for every thing that could happen next, and you label that branch with its probability. When you travel along the branches from left to right, you multiply the probabilities together, and the number at the end of a path is the chance of that whole sequence happening. All those endpoint probabilities should add up to one, because something has to happen. A couple of quick rules to keep in mind. First, branches along a path are multiplied. Second, when several different paths lead to the event you care about, you add those path probabilities together. And third, the branches coming out of the same node always add to one, because one of those outcomes is certain. For example, tossing a coin twice gives four paths, each ending at one quarter. The chance of getting exactly one head is the quarter for heads-then-tails plus the quarter for tails-then-heads, giving one half. Now let's see it in action.